  "question": {
    "text": "A musician using an open flute of length <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>50</mn><mi>&#160;</mi><mi>cm</mi></math> produces second harmonic sound waves. A person runs towards the musician from another end of a hall at a speed of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>10</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">&#160;</mi><mi>km</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h</mi><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></math>. If the wave speed is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>330</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">&#160;</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></math>, the frequency heard by the running person shall be close to"
  },
  "options": [
    {
      "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>333</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">&#160;</mi><mi>Hz</mi></math>",
      "is_correct": false
    },
    {
      "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>500</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">&#160;</mi><mi>Hz</mi></math>",
      "is_correct": false
    },
    {
      "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>666</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">&#160;</mi><mi>Hz</mi></math>",
      "is_correct": true
    },
    {
      "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>753</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">&#160;</mi><mi>Hz</mi></math>",
      "is_correct": false
    }
  ],
  "correct_answer": [
    "C"
  ],
  "solution": {
    "text": "<blockquote><p>Frequency of the observer,&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#215;</mo><mfrac><mi>v</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>l</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>330</mn><mn>0.5</mn></mfrac><mi>&#160;</mi><mi>Hz</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>660</mn><mi>&#160;</mi><mi>Hz</mi></math><br /><br />Velocity of person<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>=</mo><mn>10</mn><mi>&#160;</mi><mi>km</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h</mi><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>10</mn><mo>&#215;</mo><mfrac><mn>5</mn><mn>18</mn></mfrac><mi>&#160;</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>2.8</mn><mi>&#160;</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></math><br />According to Doppler effect, frequency heard by the person,<br /><br /><math><mi>f</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mrow><mi>f</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>o</mi></mrow></msub><mfrac><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>+</mo><msub><mrow><mi>v</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mrow><mi>v</mi></mrow></mfrac></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>f</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>660</mn><mo>&#215;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>330</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>2.8</mn></mrow><mn>330</mn></mfrac><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">&#160;</mi><mi>Hz</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>666</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">&#160;</mi><mi>Hz</mi></math></p></blockquote>"
  }
